     same way (useAiStream against the registered backend route,
     computed-disabled button, no placeholder strings).

8) Off-mode invariant baseline:
   - guard.New(settings).Wrap("preheat-precool-recommender", h)
     returns 404 BEFORE h sees the request (proven by F0/F2
     foundation tests).
   - withAiFeature('preheat-precool-recommender', InnerSection)
     returns null when ai_mode='off' OR when the per-feature
     toggle is false; the wrapper carries
     data-testid="ai-feature-preheat-precool-recommender-root"
     so the off-mode + on-mode tests can prove
     absence/presence by test ID.

=== REASONING ===

1) Default-off (ADR-015 §I1): registry entry has DefaultOn: false.
   The user must explicitly toggle the feature in Settings ⇒ AI
   to surface the section. No code path renders the AI surface
   unless ai_mode != 'off' AND the toggle is true.

2) Hidden UI (ADR-015 §I5) + 404 routes (§I6): the AI section is
   gated by withAiFeature; the backend route is gated by
   guard.Wrap. Both gates fail closed in off mode, returning
   nothing in the DOM and 404 from the API respectively.

3) Baseline coexistence (ADR-015 §I3, slice prompt P10): the
   ClimateControlPage's deterministic content (HVAC banner,
   status grid, efficiency panel, history, seat heaters, manual
   heuristic) is UNCHANGED. The AI section is layered above the
   HVAC banner (visually inserted before the status cards) so
   off-mode users see exactly what they saw before this slice.
   The AI section never replaces the canonical Save/Apply UI —
   the user must click the existing manual climate controls
   Apply button to persist any AI proposal (propose-only, §I8).

4) Per-feature opt-in (ADR-015 §I7): the per-feature toggle in
   Settings ⇒ AI controls visibility independently of the
   ai_mode global. Both the off-mode (mode=off) and toggle-off
   (mode=cloud + toggle=false) tests prove the feature is hidden
   when EITHER gate is closed.

5) Type system (ADR-015 §I10): the AI surface lives entirely
   under /api/v1/ai/*. No field on the existing
   /api/v1/vehicles/{id}/climate-state or
   /api/v1/vehicles/{id}/climate-history JSON shapes is added
   or modified. The backend handler accepts a typed shape
   (vehicle_id, depart_by, current_cabin_temp_c, outside_temp_c,
   target_cabin_temp_c) and returns SSE frames; no DB row is
   written.

6) Decorator chain (P5): the dispatch.Run path used by this
   handler runs through the same provider.Registry.For(...) loop
   used by every other slice. The locked decorator order
   (redaction → rate limit → cost cap → audit → trace) is
   inherited from the registry-wide construction in router.go.
   No bespoke decorator stack is created here.

7) Tool runtime (P4 / F4): both tools have Mutates() == false.
   draft_climate_schedule delegates to the
   AIClimateScheduleAdvisor (a pure-Go deterministic departure
   heuristic — no DB, no Tesla API call). validate_climate_schedule
   re-checks the typed envelope (start<end, end<=depart_by,
   target in [10,32]°C, mode/delta direction match). The LLM
   never writes raw SQL and never bypasses existing handlers.

8) Redaction (P5 / I9): PolicyPreheatPrecoolRecommender installed
   with Allow=[ClassVehicleName] and Mode=ModeRedactedTags. The
   round-trip is required (redact on outbound, restore on
   inbound). Cabin temperatures and timestamps are not PII so
   they are not in any redaction class.

9) Streamed narrative (P9 / F9): the handler opens an SSE stream
   via internal/ai/stream.New BEFORE the dispatcher runs, and
   the dispatcher's deferred WriteDone closes it. The frontend
   uses the canonical useAiStream hook with a body that mirrors
   the typed parser. No bespoke EventSource shim is added.

10) Allowed-files boundaries:
    - The slice prompt's allow list mentions
      `web/src/features/climate/**`, but that directory does not
      exist; the climate page lives under
      `web/src/features/vehicle-systems/`. Per the Predecessor
      0030 precedent (which also edited the page in the
      "owning" feature directory rather than a non-existent
      path), the actual page edit lands in
      `web/src/features/vehicle-systems/pages/ClimateControlPage.tsx`
      and the sibling tests in
      `web/src/features/vehicle-systems/__tests__/`. This is
      consistent with the slice's intent: the AI surface mounts
      on the canonical climate page, wherever that page actually
      lives.
    - `internal/ai/redact/policies.go` — every wired slice adds
      a per-feature policy here (predecessor 0030 did the same).
      This file is the canonical home for redaction policies and
      is implicitly in scope.
    - `web/src/ai/features.ts` — auto-regenerated from the Go
      registry by `go run ./tools/aigen`. Per F1/F2 foundation
      contract, this file MUST be in sync with
      `internal/ai/features/registry.go` (aigen --check enforces
      this); regenerating it is mechanical, not a separate edit.
    - `web/src/App.tsx` — adding the /climate route alias is
      mandated by the slice prompt's "Frontend routes: /climate"
      line; the existing `/climate-control` route stays as an
      alias for backward compatibility. Predecessor 0030 made
      the equivalent edit for /vampire-drain.
    - `web/src/i18n/ar.json` and `he.json` — these files are
      placeholder files by design (their `_meta.note` says all
      keys fall back to `en.json` via i18next's `fallbackLng`).
      Predecessor slices 0026–0030 followed the same convention
      and did not add per-key entries; this slice inherits the
      same fallback contract. The toggle copy in en.json is
      reachable from every locale via fallback.

